00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.390
Let's first look at what you
might have done in the past.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:09.699
So when you visualize traffic,
there's lots of tools around.

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:13.000
Open source and
commercial tools,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.760
they all kind of
do the same thing.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.330
They read some
counters over SNMP,

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:24.280
the aggregate them, they
take out overflow data.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:25.900
And then they visualize
them and store

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.270
all the data in a database.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:31.060
And that's how you
visualize layer two data.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:34.810
So you can't get
anything about protocols.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:36.370
If you want to dig
deeper, you need

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:38.890
to use any of these
flow-like protocols.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:42.940
Netflow, jFlow, sFlow,
more recently, IPFIX.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.820
And they also all kind
of work the same way.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:51.520
You have a router or
switch exporting flow data.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.330
And you have a couple of
collectors who take that data

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:56.560
and store it in a database.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.470
And then you have some tools
to analyze the data around.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:04.540
What's a little bit difficult
is getting 100% coverage.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:08.920
So in this example here, you
can see we collect the data

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.690
right next to the firewall.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.310
So while everything
going in and out

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:14.740
is captured, if
you don't capture

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:17.290
data between the servers.

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:21.010
In other ways, when you
have a lot of exporters,

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:22.870
you might capture
some flow as double,

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.800
and then you have to
subtract them up again,

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.780
so it's kind of a
little bit difficult.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:32.040
So once you do collect the
data, what do people do with it?

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.920
And we see a few
common use cases there.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.740
The first one everyone first
looks at desktop top talkers.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.980
You see in your
network who generates

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.930
the most traffic,
outside of your network,

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.290
where does it go
to find anomalies

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:46.950
or something like that.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.290
Some people use it
for capacity planning,

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.480
because you get
autonomous system numbers,

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.300
you can decide who might
you want to peer with,

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:57.730
where do you need to augment
capacity, something like that.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.660
And in the security
area, people might

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:04.890
look at anomalies, small packet
sizes, lots of UDP traffic.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:09.240
And they either do that through
some specialized tools or they

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:14.120
export all the data in the same
system they're using across.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:18.300
Last use case we commonly
see is to verify other data.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.019
So I have been seeing an ISPs
where they look at NetFlow data

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:26.040
to see if their bill they get
from another ISP is correct.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.220
It's not something good
enough and granular enough

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.590
to actually create a
bill, but you can double

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:35.860
check data you generate
in another way.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.780
So that's how you might
have done it on premises.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:41.150
Now how does map to the cloud?

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:43.870
What data do you
have available there?

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:46.690
Let's first look at the
interface traffic again.

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:48.910
And you might not
have found a tab,

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:53.050
but actually right in the GCP
console, if you go to a VM,

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:57.160
there's network tab and you
can visualize bytes and packets

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:57.970
for every VM.

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:01.480
So quite similar to what
you've seen on the first slide

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:03.020
in on premises.

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:05.590
With Stackdriver
monitoring, you can

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:08.120
create these aggregate
graphs you might get used to.

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.330
So that's quite similar as well.

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:12.130
But you might want to
use your own tools.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:14.020
You might want to export that.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:17.740
And Stackdriver monitoring also
has Stackdriver monitoring API,

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:20.920
so you can export that data
to any tool you'd want,

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:24.040
either your existing ones
or you have something new.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:27.540
And if you ever wrote
any tool that used SNMP,

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.750
you will be very happy to
not anymore use it, but just

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:32.080
use RESTful API instead.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:38.184
Now there's also
elements in networking

00:05:38.184 --> 00:05:39.850
which you have in
cloud, which you don't

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:41.590
have the same way on premises.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:42.910
Like our global load balancer.

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:45.850
You don't have a global
balancing element in your

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:47.080
on premise network.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.870
And we also can export
some data there.

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:54.520
Similar in the console, we can
show you quite some nice graphs

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:57.070
of where does your
traffic come from, which

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:59.410
backend group does it go to.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:01.840
But if you actually go into
this Stackdriver monitoring,

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.310
you can get further metrics.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.860
Like we can see
the round trip time

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:09.370
on the front end and the total
round trip time of TCP flows.

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:12.450
And you can use them right
there to create alerts,

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:15.364
or you can export
them through the API.

00:06:15.364 --> 00:06:17.280
We also have a regional
load balancer product.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.988
The metrics there are
slightly different,

00:06:18.988 --> 00:06:22.220
but again, you get some
data around the load

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:26.000
balance through Stackdriver
monitoring, and its API.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.310
So that looks quite good.

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:30.070
But of what about flow data?

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:35.360
Well, I'm pleased to say that we
recently launched VPC Flow Logs

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:36.130
NGA.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:40.160
So that's our flow-level
network telemetry product.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:41.780
So what does that mean?

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:45.300
It means every five
seconds all the TCP and UDP

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:48.890
flows get locked and
sampled and exported

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:51.945
to Stackdriver logging.

00:06:51.945 --> 00:06:52.820
So what does it mean?

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:53.980
Is it like NetFlow?

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:57.020
Well, it's like NetFlow,
but it's not NetFlow.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:58.940
So what's different?

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:01.450
First of all, we have
some exciting features

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:03.370
you don't have in NetFlow.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:05.680
We can export the
exact IP of the flow.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:09.160
We don't limit you to a subnet
[? selection ?] default level.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:11.980
And we add a lot of
useful annotations to it,

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:13.770
so you don't have to
do that to yourself.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:17.860
Like the name of the instance,
the name of the VPC subnet,

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:20.360
the regions done, et
cetera on the site.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:24.120
And some more, which I will
go into a little bit later.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.060
But not everything which
is there in NetFlow

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:27.900
is there in VPC Flow Logs.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:31.530
First of all, we capture
by flow and not by packet.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.770
So we can't give you
the packet tether

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:37.360
We didn't see too many people
actually using the full packet

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:41.300
tether, so this seems to be OK.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.970
People also like the AS numbers.

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:46.820
They wanted to see peer
end destination AS.

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:49.560
Well, you might not really
care about who you appear with,

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:52.040
so let's not care what peer AS.

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:55.040
But since people really
want the destination AS,

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:57.770
this is an annotation
we recently added.

00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:59.210
So you can now see
the destination

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:04.000
AS number right into the Flow
Log as one of the annotations.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.480
But also not the same.

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:07.390
Obviously, it's a
different log format,

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:09.056
and we have all this
useful annotations,

00:08:09.056 --> 00:08:11.140
so you can't use the
existing collectors.

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:13.790
You need to build a
different data pipeline.

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:16.520
But we do have exports
right out of box

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:19.130
to BigQuery, Cloud
Storage and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:21.040
And you can create
alerts on Stackdriver

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:22.400
that's right for it.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:24.410
And through these
tools, you can easily

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:26.060
integrate with other
topology tools.

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:28.740
I'm also going to show that.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:31.870
Let's look at the packet
format a little bit in detail.

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:34.440
And I say it's
enriched by default.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.990
Like other providers
might give you

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:38.549
like a single line
of a log, which

00:08:38.549 --> 00:08:40.799
gives you a source and
destination IP and a little bit

00:08:40.799 --> 00:08:41.669
more.

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:43.500
We give you rich
json of information

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:47.280
with all these annotations, like
the instance name and subnet

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:47.970
name.

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:52.980
But also stuff like the round
trip time on every TCP flow,

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:55.710
because we can infer
that from seeing packets

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:57.740
going in both direction.

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:01.260
Or the location of
the external entity

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:04.020
on the city level, country
level or continent level.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:05.310
So that's very, very useful.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:07.440
And some data you
might otherwise

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:13.800
have to collect and add in a
very complex way to your data.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:16.010
And you have full
coverage by default.

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:21.660
You don't have to care anymore
where to put your exporters.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:28.080
All the traffic within the
VPC is by default, exported.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:29.890
Traffic coming in
through interconnect,

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:34.740
through VPN, through the
internet directly to a VM

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.967
or through a load
balancer and to

00:09:36.967 --> 00:09:39.390
and from other Google services.

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:42.250
So you see it all.

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:45.504
Now you have the data,
what can you do with it?

00:09:45.504 --> 00:09:47.670
First of all, through the
integration with BigQuery,

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.170
it's very easy to do
quick visualizations.

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:52.571
And I'm going to demo
this with Data Studio.

00:09:52.571 --> 00:09:54.445
Other people might use
something like Tableau

00:09:54.445 --> 00:09:58.670
or anything else which
integrates in that area.

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:00.880
You can also build
complex data pipelines.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:02.890
You can use Cloud Data Flow.

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:06.500
If you like Hadoop, you
can use Dataproc instead.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:09.220
Or if you don't
use it that often,

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:11.800
you can just query
that data ad hoc

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:15.700
and build queries in BigQuery
whenever you need it.

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:19.150
And in the security space,
we see two use cases.

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:21.550
Either you export that
data through PubsHub

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:24.200
into your CYME software
and we tried out a few,

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:27.210
it works quite easily.

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:29.950
Or again, when you have
a security incident,

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:32.800
you can easily just through
the BigQuery interface,

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:36.990
dig into your data and find
out what might be going wrong.

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:38.160
But let's demo this.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:39.570
Switch to the demo please.

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:46.180
Can we switch to the demo?

00:10:52.816 --> 00:10:55.150
Not yet, there we go.

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:57.250
All right, so I have
an application built

00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:59.380
and it has a global
load balancer,

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:02.650
and it's running in
three continents,

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:04.960
and it's a simple
web application.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:07.430
So let's start
exporting flow logs.

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:09.180
So all I need to do
is select a subnetwork

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:11.050
where I want to
export flow logs.

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:12.782
So I select the US.

00:11:12.782 --> 00:11:17.110
I added it, I toggle the
flow logs, and actually

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:19.720
tells me right away how
much data that will generate

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:21.610
in Stackdriver logging.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:22.540
So let's save this.

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:26.980
And within a few
seconds, it will actually

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:29.700
start generating that
logging information.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:32.550
So let's go out to
Stackdriver logging

00:11:32.550 --> 00:11:34.240
and show the life
tale of the data.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:38.340
So you can see all
these logs coming in

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:42.240
right away on what kind flows
might be generated there.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:46.890
And you can look at the
json data, which I've seen,

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:49.930
so that works right
out of the box.

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:53.880
But looking then in Stackdriver
logging, not too useful

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:56.320
once you want to do analysis.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:00.030
So let's create an export
of that data to BigQuery.

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:05.780
Let's call it VPC
flow logs next.

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:08.100
I say I going to
create it to BigQuery

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:17.330
and I create a new
dataset, VPC flows, create.

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:21.340
So when I create that
thing, within let's say

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:24.500
a minute or two, that gets
streamed into BigQuery

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:27.740
and you can query it right
away, all the data coming in.

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:29.710
It's nearly immediate.

00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:33.430
However, for demo purposes,
a minute is quite long.

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:36.110
And also having a half a
minute or a minute of data

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:39.020
is not so interesting
to look at,

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:41.770
so I started this
expert about a month ago

00:12:41.770 --> 00:12:44.080
and have a live traffic
going on in there.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:47.500
So let's see what
I can do with this.

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:50.410
I created a query with
typical top talker.

