JENS KUEHLERS:
Thank you, Arunima.

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:18.540
So topology's
important for everyone,

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:20.340
and it always has been.

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:23.800
And unfortunately, always has
been difficult to visualize.

00:24:23.800 --> 00:24:27.900
So if you look at on
premise, the first problem

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:29.640
starts with collecting the data.

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:31.680
Like what do you
have in your network?

00:24:31.680 --> 00:24:33.180
You might use a
lot of [INAUDIBLE],,

00:24:33.180 --> 00:24:34.770
different passwords, et cetera.

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:36.960
So it's not actually
easy to get an inventory.

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:39.900
So I said you need to
use all the protocols.

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:42.980
Cisco's Discovery Protocol,
Link Layer Discovery Protocol,

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:46.620
see what you can reach for, your
SNMP community, go through ARP

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:49.770
tables, IP addresses and
hopefully, somewhere you get

00:24:49.770 --> 00:24:51.910
an inventory of your network.

00:24:51.910 --> 00:24:54.540
The good thing is,
in Computer Engine,

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:58.110
through the sub APIs
of Compute Engine API,

00:24:58.110 --> 00:24:59.160
you have the inventory.

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:00.600
It's all in Compute Engine.

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:02.730
So you have all that data.

00:25:02.730 --> 00:25:05.490
The issue is,
context is missing.

00:25:05.490 --> 00:25:07.410
You don't know how
to interrelate it.

00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:10.830
So let's see how
we've worked on that.

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:11.910
First, on premises.

00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:14.130
Let's say you collected
all that data.

00:25:14.130 --> 00:25:16.590
You might want to get a nice
network map like the one

00:25:16.590 --> 00:25:18.970
pictured on the left.

00:25:18.970 --> 00:25:23.490
But in reality, there's lots
of tools that are on topology.

00:25:23.490 --> 00:25:26.599
Every network monitoring
suite say they have it,

00:25:26.599 --> 00:25:28.140
but they're not
really focused on it.

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:31.760
So mostly you end up with a
graph little bit like that,

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:35.250
something auto generated and
it's not really what you want.

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:37.620
So what do people do?

00:25:37.620 --> 00:25:39.580
Well, you do it yourself.

00:25:39.580 --> 00:25:42.930
Most people draw network
diagrams, they draw them again,

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:45.370
they try to make them
better, they customize them,

00:25:45.370 --> 00:25:47.850
they show them to your
peers, they have them rated,

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:50.930
they make another
view, another layer.

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:54.490
And they're highly
customized and opinionated.

00:25:54.490 --> 00:25:57.750
And then in cloud, people
tried the same thing,

00:25:57.750 --> 00:25:59.800
but there's two
issues with that.

00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:01.300
First of all, it
changes so much.

00:26:01.300 --> 00:26:04.000
So you just finished
drawing your diagram,

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:05.160
your network changed again.

00:26:05.160 --> 00:26:08.460
Someone deployed something,
it's not up to date anymore.

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:11.190
The second issue is actually
how resources are organized

00:26:11.190 --> 00:26:13.050
in the cloud and on premises.

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:15.510
On premises, you have
quite a clear structure.

00:26:15.510 --> 00:26:18.770
If data comes from the internet,
it goes through several layers,

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:21.420
several aggregation layers
to top of [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:21.420 --> 00:26:24.060
until it hits like you
web application server.

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:26.640
So you have some
grouping right there.

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:29.310
If you look in the cloud,
you have a software

00:26:29.310 --> 00:26:32.580
defined network running on
Andromeda through a network

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:33.690
fabric.

00:26:33.690 --> 00:26:36.080
Everything is kind of
close to each other.

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:37.890
You talk through
that virtual switch.

00:26:37.890 --> 00:26:39.190
You have a flat network.

00:26:39.190 --> 00:26:41.610
So how to you actually
do that diagram?

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:44.410
Some people try grouping by
subnet or something else.

00:26:44.410 --> 00:26:45.750
But it's not inherent.

00:26:45.750 --> 00:26:48.087
So the structure is
a little bit missing.

00:26:48.087 --> 00:26:49.670
And that's what's
also the first thing

00:26:49.670 --> 00:26:54.480
we've seen when we tried to
look what we can do for you.

00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:57.690
And what we made out of
the research of Arunima

00:26:57.690 --> 00:27:01.950
and those different realities we
have, I'm going to invite Greg,

00:27:01.950 --> 00:27:02.730
and he tell you.

00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:08.130
GREG FRANCZYK: OK, so let's
take a moment and recap.

00:27:08.130 --> 00:27:10.890
So what we heard from
you was pretty clear.

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:13.500
You want to see
relationships, and then also

00:27:13.500 --> 00:27:15.220
need a way to layer
meaningful insights

00:27:15.220 --> 00:27:17.050
on top of those relationships.

00:27:17.050 --> 00:27:20.070
However, as Jens just mentioned,
visualizing cloud topologies

00:27:20.070 --> 00:27:21.330
is not straightforward.

00:27:21.330 --> 00:27:22.782
Because the cloud is a mesh.

00:27:22.782 --> 00:27:24.490
Everything is talking
to everything else.

00:27:24.490 --> 00:27:25.351
It's like a web.

00:27:25.351 --> 00:27:26.850
If you remember the
bundle of cables

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:28.364
that Jens had on
the screen earlier,

00:27:28.364 --> 00:27:30.030
that's basically what
it looks like when

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:33.720
you visualize 1,000 instances
all talking to themselves.

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:35.460
We're missing
intermediate devices.

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.800
Those intermediate
devices add structure

00:27:37.800 --> 00:27:39.870
and make your topology
look nice, clean,

00:27:39.870 --> 00:27:44.050
and allow you to
navigate it effectively.

00:27:44.050 --> 00:27:47.270
So why is that a problem?

00:27:47.270 --> 00:27:50.020
Well, without those intermediate
devices, you have that web,

00:27:50.020 --> 00:27:51.936
you have so many things
talking to each other,

00:27:51.936 --> 00:27:53.750
and you can't
effectively navigate.

00:27:53.750 --> 00:27:56.730
However, those intermediate
devices also in a way

00:27:56.730 --> 00:28:00.190
structure you into looking
at your topology in a very

00:28:00.190 --> 00:28:02.350
specific and a very unique way.

00:28:02.350 --> 00:28:04.104
You look at switches,
you look at servers

00:28:04.104 --> 00:28:05.770
that are attached the
switches, you look

00:28:05.770 --> 00:28:08.260
at core switches and firewalls.

00:28:08.260 --> 00:28:10.150
And you can't
necessarily navigate

00:28:10.150 --> 00:28:13.630
in the way that's natural to
you and the way you'd like to.

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:16.240
So we realized when
we took a step back,

00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:18.280
is that we have an
organizational problem.

00:28:18.280 --> 00:28:20.470
And we were using intermediate
devices historically

00:28:20.470 --> 00:28:23.380
here in order to fill this
gap of organizing the data

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:26.170
and allowing you to
navigate a complex network.

00:28:26.170 --> 00:28:28.150
So we thought about
an alternate mechanism

00:28:28.150 --> 00:28:31.960
of structuring the data in a way
that allows you to navigate it.

00:28:31.960 --> 00:28:34.030
And a natural way
was hierarchies.

00:28:34.030 --> 00:28:36.070
If you take a group
of resources and group

00:28:36.070 --> 00:28:37.960
them together, and
you take those groups

00:28:37.960 --> 00:28:40.889
and put them into higher
groups and so on and so forth,

00:28:40.889 --> 00:28:43.180
you can get to a point where
you have a very clean view

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:45.513
and then you can drill down
and find exactly what you're

00:28:45.513 --> 00:28:46.300
looking for.

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:49.210
The ability to select
entities and the ability

00:28:49.210 --> 00:28:52.570
to put them into hierarchies
is a key organizational concern

00:28:52.570 --> 00:28:54.820
that's necessary in order
to visualize topologies

00:28:54.820 --> 00:28:56.320
effectively.

00:28:56.320 --> 00:28:59.826
Unfortunately, there's no clean
hierarchy for most resources.

00:28:59.826 --> 00:29:01.450
It's unfortunate in
one way, because it

00:29:01.450 --> 00:29:03.020
makes our lives a lot harder.

00:29:03.020 --> 00:29:05.020
But it's actually very
fortunate for you.

00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:07.210
Because that means you
can group things together

00:29:07.210 --> 00:29:08.530
in many different ways.

00:29:08.530 --> 00:29:12.140
An instance, for instance,
could be in a group.

00:29:12.140 --> 00:29:13.270
It could be in a zone.

00:29:13.270 --> 00:29:15.462
It could be directly in
a region or in a project.

00:29:15.462 --> 00:29:17.170
There's a lot of
different ways to do it.

00:29:17.170 --> 00:29:19.180
And rather than
saying we'll create

00:29:19.180 --> 00:29:21.460
one hierarchy for
every base resource,

00:29:21.460 --> 00:29:24.610
we decided to create
many hierarchies.

00:29:24.610 --> 00:29:27.492
Before I give you a demo of the
thing that we're building now,

00:29:27.492 --> 00:29:29.200
I want to explain a
few key concepts that

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:31.780
are key to this
organizational structure.

00:29:31.780 --> 00:29:33.640
So first, we thought
about base entities.

00:29:33.640 --> 00:29:36.181
Based entities are the things
that you're very familiar with.

00:29:36.181 --> 00:29:37.870
Things that communicate
on the network.

00:29:37.870 --> 00:29:39.310
In an on prem
world, this would be

00:29:39.310 --> 00:29:40.600
a server with a network card.

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:43.780
The network card in our
world would be a base entity.

00:29:43.780 --> 00:29:45.580
But also things like
your external clients,

00:29:45.580 --> 00:29:49.090
things that have IP addresses
outside of your GCP network.

00:29:49.090 --> 00:29:52.660
Your on prem, so
your VPN tunnels,

00:29:52.660 --> 00:29:55.020
your routers on
prem, the things that

00:29:55.020 --> 00:29:57.330
your interconnect
attachments point to,

00:29:57.330 --> 00:29:59.080
are all base entities.

00:29:59.080 --> 00:30:02.060
We took those base entities,
and then we figured out, OK,

00:30:02.060 --> 00:30:03.976
what are hierarchies
that make sense?

00:30:03.976 --> 00:30:05.600
So hierarchies, as
I mentioned earlier,

00:30:05.600 --> 00:30:07.308
are just groupings of
those base entities

00:30:07.308 --> 00:30:08.410
that go higher and higher.

00:30:08.410 --> 00:30:11.290
And then a hierarchy family
is a collection of hierarchies

00:30:11.290 --> 00:30:13.250
that all ends at the
same base entity.

00:30:13.250 --> 00:30:16.210
So if you have a instance,
you can have many hierarchies,

00:30:16.210 --> 00:30:17.890
and they all form
a hierarchy family.

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:22.510
These are key navigational
concepts that are important.

00:30:22.510 --> 00:30:25.330
So we've organized the data and
we created a structure for it.

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:26.570
So now what can we do?

00:30:26.570 --> 00:30:28.210
Well, you saw VPC
flow logs before.

00:30:28.210 --> 00:30:30.940
We also have HTTP
load balancer logs

00:30:30.940 --> 00:30:32.440
that are in Stackdriver today.

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:34.662
We took this data and we
started to process it.

00:30:34.662 --> 00:30:36.370
And what we were
looking for in this data

00:30:36.370 --> 00:30:38.570
was relationships and metrics.

00:30:38.570 --> 00:30:40.810
The same things that you
identified and let us

00:30:40.810 --> 00:30:43.910
know that you really needed.

00:30:43.910 --> 00:30:45.220
So let's put this all together.

00:30:45.220 --> 00:30:47.020
We created meaningful
hierarchies and a way

00:30:47.020 --> 00:30:48.280
to structure the data.

00:30:48.280 --> 00:30:50.970
We took relationships
out of the VPC flow

00:30:50.970 --> 00:30:54.160
logs and the load balancer logs
that were already receiving.

00:30:54.160 --> 00:30:55.840
And then we skimmed
insights off of that

00:30:55.840 --> 00:30:57.550
as well, actual metrics.

00:30:57.550 --> 00:30:58.990
And then we applied
those metrics

00:30:58.990 --> 00:31:02.420
on top of those relationships.

00:31:02.420 --> 00:31:04.990
So I'm going to do a demo of
a project that we're building,

00:31:04.990 --> 00:31:06.552
of a product that
we're building now.

00:31:06.552 --> 00:31:09.010
Before I get into the demo, I
think a little bit of context

00:31:09.010 --> 00:31:10.230
is necessary.

00:31:10.230 --> 00:31:13.462
So in our demo, we have a
worldwide e-commerce site.

00:31:13.462 --> 00:31:15.670
This e-commerce site is made
up of fundamentally four

00:31:15.670 --> 00:31:16.960
different applications.

00:31:16.960 --> 00:31:19.290
There's a browse component,
a cart component, checkout

00:31:19.290 --> 00:31:20.370
and feeds.

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:22.756
It's globally distributed
in three GCP regions.

00:31:22.756 --> 00:31:24.130
It's a worldwide
e-commerce shop,

00:31:24.130 --> 00:31:26.140
so that's important to them.

00:31:26.140 --> 00:31:28.534
And the data has to be
replicated around the world.

00:31:28.534 --> 00:31:29.950
So this particular
e-commerce shop

00:31:29.950 --> 00:31:31.450
is using a database
solution that's

00:31:31.450 --> 00:31:34.930
replicating between GCP regions,
but all reads and writes

00:31:34.930 --> 00:31:36.420
are happening locally.

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:39.250
So now that you understand
a little bit about how

00:31:39.250 --> 00:31:41.860
the application is
structured, how the company's

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:43.630
infrastructure is
structured, let's

00:31:43.630 --> 00:31:45.430
go into a demo of
how you can visualize

00:31:45.430 --> 00:31:49.254
and how you can see this
structure in action.

00:31:49.254 --> 00:31:52.810
Oh, thanks.

00:31:52.810 --> 00:31:56.650
OK, so this is the product
that we are currently building.

00:31:56.650 --> 00:31:59.380
We are in an early
preview of this product.

00:31:59.380 --> 00:32:03.317
And what you see here is
the Americas, EMEA and APAC.

00:32:03.317 --> 00:32:04.900
You see a load
balancer in the middle,

00:32:04.900 --> 00:32:07.450
and you see three GCP
regions with dotted lines

00:32:07.450 --> 00:32:09.200
moving all over the place.

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:11.470
This is a visual
representation of the project

00:32:11.470 --> 00:32:12.412
I just described.

00:32:12.412 --> 00:32:13.870
The dotted lines
are traffic that's

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:16.300
flowing between
external sources, a load

00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:17.710
balancer and regions.

00:32:17.710 --> 00:32:20.650
And this is all generated
dynamically based off

00:32:20.650 --> 00:32:23.050
of your project's configuration
and the actual things

00:32:23.050 --> 00:32:24.860
that you have going on.

00:32:24.860 --> 00:32:27.100
If this customer decided
to create a new GCP region

00:32:27.100 --> 00:32:30.700
tomorrow or not create a new
GCP region, but build into one,

00:32:30.700 --> 00:32:32.680
that would show up
instantly in this diagram.

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:35.719
So would have alternate
scales with your business.

00:32:35.719 --> 00:32:37.510
Now, this view is a
little bit complicated,

00:32:37.510 --> 00:32:39.190
because you see a
mixture of things

00:32:39.190 --> 00:32:40.898
that are coming through
the load balancer

00:32:40.898 --> 00:32:43.000
and also TCP traffic
that's coming directly

00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:46.540
from external clients and
going inside the GCP regions

00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:47.506
and between them.

00:32:47.506 --> 00:32:48.880
Let's take a look
at a view where

00:32:48.880 --> 00:32:53.080
we're looking specifically
at just a load balancer.

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:55.420
So here, you've got
three business regions.

00:32:55.420 --> 00:32:56.804
Americas, EMEA and APAC.

00:32:56.804 --> 00:32:58.720
And you've got a load
balancer sending traffic

00:32:58.720 --> 00:33:00.803
to the three regions, just
like on the other view,

00:33:00.803 --> 00:33:03.460
except there's a lot less lines
and it's a little bit cleaner.

00:33:03.460 --> 00:33:07.810
So now when we look at this,
we can tell specifically

00:33:07.810 --> 00:33:10.061
how this traffic is being
distributed by the balancer.

00:33:10.061 --> 00:33:12.143
Now let's go through a
load case that's important,

00:33:12.143 --> 00:33:13.330
a use case that's important.

00:33:13.330 --> 00:33:16.300
As a network admin, I might be
concerned about whether or not

00:33:16.300 --> 00:33:19.575
traffic is going to the
closest region to the customer.

00:33:19.575 --> 00:33:20.950
The lowest latency
will always be

00:33:20.950 --> 00:33:24.166
achieved if the application
that's in that region

00:33:24.166 --> 00:33:25.540
is serving those
requests, and it

00:33:25.540 --> 00:33:28.040
doesn't have to travel
globally around the world.

00:33:28.040 --> 00:33:31.100
So if I hover over APAC, I'll
see some interesting things.

00:33:31.100 --> 00:33:33.100
First of all, I'll see
some metrics on the edge.

00:33:33.100 --> 00:33:34.120
I know they're
hard to read there,

00:33:34.120 --> 00:33:35.495
but it shows that
we're receiving

00:33:35.495 --> 00:33:38.020
12 kilobytes of traffic
into the load balancer,

00:33:38.020 --> 00:33:41.319
and it's sending 60
kilobytes a second.

00:33:41.319 --> 00:33:43.360
On top of that, I can see
that all traffic that's

00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:45.850
coming from APAC, is going
directly to the Asia East

00:33:45.850 --> 00:33:49.730
1 region, which means that this
is set up properly, looks good.

00:33:49.730 --> 00:33:50.620
OK, what's next?

00:33:50.620 --> 00:33:51.730
Let's take a look at EMEA.

00:33:51.730 --> 00:33:53.110
EMEA, same thing.

00:33:53.110 --> 00:33:54.800
I see some metrics on this edge.

00:33:54.800 --> 00:33:56.800
How much is going to the
load balancer, and then

00:33:56.800 --> 00:33:58.258
I see it's all
traveling to Europe.

00:33:58.258 --> 00:34:00.680
But what happens if
I click on Americas?

00:34:00.680 --> 00:34:03.520
Ah, in this scenario, I actually
see traffic from the Americas

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:05.240
is going into two
different places.

00:34:05.240 --> 00:34:09.457
One, into US Central 1,
and also into Asia East 1.

00:34:09.457 --> 00:34:11.540
That's an indicator to the
network admin that hey,

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:12.380
something is wrong here.

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:13.449
This is not optimized.

00:34:13.449 --> 00:34:15.250
If my customers in
the US have traffic

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:17.320
being served by
resources in Asia,

00:34:17.320 --> 00:34:19.120
it's not optimal for them.

00:34:19.120 --> 00:34:21.216
If I open up Asia,
I can take a look

00:34:21.216 --> 00:34:22.840
at the different
groups that are there.

00:34:22.840 --> 00:34:25.131
I mentioned there was a browse
group, a checkout group,

00:34:25.131 --> 00:34:26.070
you see them all here.

00:34:26.070 --> 00:34:27.634
I go back, I click
on Americas, and I

00:34:27.634 --> 00:34:29.800
see that the traffic that's
flowing through the load

00:34:29.800 --> 00:34:33.880
balancer is flowing to just
one of those groups in Asia.

00:34:33.880 --> 00:34:36.370
If I open up the US
central 1 region,

00:34:36.370 --> 00:34:38.330
I see traffic's
flowing to all of them.

00:34:38.330 --> 00:34:39.969
So this is an
indicator to me that

00:34:39.969 --> 00:34:41.920
this particular
backend service, which

00:34:41.920 --> 00:34:43.679
is made up of three
instance groups,

00:34:43.679 --> 00:34:46.090
is sending traffic
to two, instead

00:34:46.090 --> 00:34:50.729
of the one that's most closely
correlated with the user.

00:34:50.729 --> 00:34:52.270
Now that I have this
view, I can also

00:34:52.270 --> 00:34:54.429
use it to pull more information.

00:34:54.429 --> 00:34:56.020
So I can open up
the load balancer.

00:34:56.020 --> 00:34:57.820
And inside the load
balancer, we see

00:34:57.820 --> 00:34:59.290
these are the different
backend services that

00:34:59.290 --> 00:35:01.373
match the applications we
were just talking about.

00:35:04.060 --> 00:35:05.560
I can click on one
of the backends

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:08.080
and get a time series graph
right on the side, user stacked

00:35:08.080 --> 00:35:12.280
driver metrics that show request
count and back and latencies

00:35:12.280 --> 00:35:14.575
for that backend service.

00:35:14.575 --> 00:35:16.450
Because this is in
Stackdriver, because these

00:35:16.450 --> 00:35:18.160
are all Stackdriver
metrics, these

00:35:18.160 --> 00:35:19.884
are things you
can set alerts on.

00:35:19.884 --> 00:35:22.300
You can use these views to not
only discover your network,

00:35:22.300 --> 00:35:24.910
not only see metrics,
but then also use

00:35:24.910 --> 00:35:27.220
alerting in order
to operationalize

00:35:27.220 --> 00:35:29.330
what you're doing.

00:35:29.330 --> 00:35:31.360
Let's go through
another use case.

00:35:31.360 --> 00:35:34.370
Since this is a globally
distributed business,

00:35:34.370 --> 00:35:36.340
we have traffic moving
between GCP regions.

00:35:36.340 --> 00:35:39.460
And as many of you know, traffic
moving between GCP regions

00:35:39.460 --> 00:35:40.630
is costly.

00:35:40.630 --> 00:35:41.810
It's costly in two ways.

00:35:41.810 --> 00:35:44.500
One, if your application
can't read and write locally,

00:35:44.500 --> 00:35:47.050
it has to pay a latency
penalty to send traffic

00:35:47.050 --> 00:35:48.100
across the world.

00:35:48.100 --> 00:35:50.230
But two, it's
actually money costly.

00:35:50.230 --> 00:35:51.789
It has an impact on your bill.

00:35:51.789 --> 00:35:54.330
So we want to make sure that
all the traffic that's moving in

00:35:54.330 --> 00:35:56.740
between GCP regions
is traffic that's

00:35:56.740 --> 00:35:59.840
only related to the databases.

00:35:59.840 --> 00:36:02.522
No applications are reading
and writing across the world.

00:36:02.522 --> 00:36:04.480
So let's see how we can
do that with this view.

00:36:04.480 --> 00:36:06.640
First, I can hover
over a region and see

00:36:06.640 --> 00:36:08.740
how much traffic is moving
in between the region

00:36:08.740 --> 00:36:09.660
and other regions.

00:36:09.660 --> 00:36:11.410
If you've ever had to
debug an issue with,

00:36:11.410 --> 00:36:14.020
why is my bill so high for
it to reach in traffic,

00:36:14.020 --> 00:36:17.650
this would save you a
lot of time, trust me.

00:36:17.650 --> 00:36:21.190
If I open up the regions, I
can see instance groups again.

00:36:21.190 --> 00:36:23.410
Notice, in the past we
saw four instance groups.

00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:24.437
Now we see five.

00:36:24.437 --> 00:36:26.020
The fifth one is the
database servers.

00:36:26.020 --> 00:36:27.550
They're not seeing any
load balancing traffic,

00:36:27.550 --> 00:36:29.091
so they weren't in
the previous view.

00:36:29.091 --> 00:36:30.730
It wasn't important
for you there.

00:36:30.730 --> 00:36:33.640
Now if I click on, let's say
the database group in Asia,

00:36:33.640 --> 00:36:35.710
I can see that
this database group

00:36:35.710 --> 00:36:37.510
is talking only
to other database

00:36:37.510 --> 00:36:39.700
groups in other
regions, and to all

00:36:39.700 --> 00:36:41.784
the applications local to it.

00:36:41.784 --> 00:36:42.700
It's an early preview.

00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:45.033
We don't have all the metrics
yet, but the metrics we do

00:36:45.033 --> 00:36:46.200
have, you see as well.

00:36:46.200 --> 00:36:47.950
All right, so this is
the amount of volume

00:36:47.950 --> 00:36:49.564
that's coming from
the database group

00:36:49.564 --> 00:36:50.980
and going into a
different region.

00:36:50.980 --> 00:36:52.646
If there was another
instance group here

00:36:52.646 --> 00:36:55.510
also sending traffic, we could
focus on that particular group

00:36:55.510 --> 00:36:56.800
and see those metrics as well.

00:37:01.620 --> 00:37:03.640
All right, can we go back
to the slides, please?

00:37:07.720 --> 00:37:09.820
All right, so some
key takeaways.

00:37:09.820 --> 00:37:11.050
We have VPC flow logs.

00:37:11.050 --> 00:37:12.970
Those are available in GA today.

00:37:12.970 --> 00:37:15.970
They're annotated by default and
easy to integrate with Google

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:17.759
tools, and also
third party tools.

00:37:17.759 --> 00:37:19.300
We have Stackdriver
monitoring, which

00:37:19.300 --> 00:37:22.596
has been there for everything,
not only for networking.

00:37:22.596 --> 00:37:24.220
There, you can get
time series metrics,

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:26.950
you can get logging, all
networking specific as well,

00:37:26.950 --> 00:37:30.620
but also programmatic API
access to pull that information.

00:37:30.620 --> 00:37:32.616
And finally, the
network topology graph.

00:37:32.616 --> 00:37:34.240
So we're currently
in an early preview,

00:37:34.240 --> 00:37:36.670
and we're opening
for trusted testers.

00:37:36.670 --> 00:37:39.940
We're combining relationship and
metric data into a single view.

00:37:39.940 --> 00:37:41.560
And we're organizing
it by hierarchies

00:37:41.560 --> 00:37:43.810
to make it easy to consume,
and are very much focused

00:37:43.810 --> 00:37:47.825
on the use cases that you need
to solve on a day-to-day basis.

00:37:47.825 --> 00:37:51.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

